What is Network Topology?
Simple Definition
Network Topology is the way computers and network devices are connected to each other.

Easy Definition
Topology means the layout or design of a computer network.

Why do we need Network Topology?

Topology helps us decide:

How devices will connect.
How data will travel.
Network speed.
Network cost.
Ease of maintenance.
Real-Life Example

Think of roads in a city.

Different cities have different road layouts.

Similarly,

Computers can be connected in different layouts.

That layout is called Network Topology.

Types of Topology

There are mainly 6 types.

1. Bus
2. Star
3. Ring
4. Mesh
5. Tree
6. Hybrid

-------------------------------------
1. Bus Topology
Definition
In Bus Topology, all computers are connected to one main cable, called the Backbone Cable.

PC
 |

========== Backbone Cable ==========
 |
PC
 |
PC
How does it work?

Suppose:

Computer A sends data.

↓

Data travels through the backbone cable.

↓

Every computer receives it.

↓

Only the destination computer accepts it.

Real-Life Example

Think of one main road.

All houses are connected to that road.

Advantages
Cheap
Easy to install
Less cable required
Disadvantages
Backbone cable failure stops the entire network.
Slow when many computers are connected.
Difficult to find faults.
Interview Answer

Bus Topology connects all devices using one backbone cable. Data travels through this cable to all devices.

Important Points

✔ One Backbone Cable

✔ Cheapest Topology

✔ Backbone failure = Entire network stops
